{"kind":"request","text":"S4 source question: where does a Registry-relayed one-shot credential leave the private Agent owner?\n\nThe Agent checks its exact code, op/config/control, roles and clock, then returns the code without another await. The trusted Registry then awaits public identity and fresh membership roles before settling its own exact control/map/native rows. During that last role read, another allowed Agent operation can consume the code or change private config without changing the six public identity fields. Registry can then relay its held result. This proves delayed delivery; it does not prove an authority bypass, unauthorized recipient, remint, successful replay or usable revoked credential.\n\nPlease rule whether private candidate release linearizes at trusted Agent handoff, with Registry final membership judgment and local settlement, or requires a separate bounded remote candidate protocol at the later outward return. I recommend the Agent handoff boundary with an explicit delayed-delivery qualification: Registry does not promise current remote candidate validity or a lease at delivery, and every actual use still gets fresh admission. A remote re-read followed by another role await cannot atomically lock both objects.\n\nThe attached question and source pins give the exact ordering and the local contract wording that prompted it. Either answer preserves one-shot/reuse effects, newer candidates, historical nonsecret receipts, absolute deadlines and Native judgment. All S4 source remains unadopted and unassembled; H validation continues independently. No public identity/proof field, recovery path or validation body has been added for this question.","body":{"conditions":"Rule the Agent private candidate release boundary for trusted Registry relay; preserve exact local owners and fresh actual credential use without claiming cross-object atomicity.","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}